rice:IRRI_RD2018_downy-mildew_p1 rice:passageText """The disease was reported in Japan in 1911 and in Italy in 1912 by Yamada and Brizi (Ou 1985). It has also been observed on rice in Australia, northeastern and eastern mainland China, Taiwan, Arkansas USA, Korea (Jung et al 1974), and India (Dasgupta and Mukhaerjee 1976). Besides rice, the fungus has also been shown to attack a wide range of hosts, including more than 43 genera in the Gramineae family. The fungal pathogen can infect oats, barley, wheat, rye, millet, and maize (Akai and Fukutomi 1964)."""@en .
rice:IRRI_RD2018_downy-mildew_p2 rice:passageText """The disease is known as “yellow wilt” in India (Dasgupta and Mukhaerjee 1976). It adversely affects the development of individual plants but not significantly rice yield, maybe due to its occurrence at low temperatures under field conditions. The active temperature range for disease development is 15-25oC with an optimum of about 20oC or slightly lower (Yamanaka and Kawai 1961). The disease, therefore, occurs only in a temperate rice production environment where the rice crop is likely to suffer stress due to low temperature."""@en .
rice:IRRI_RD2018_downy-mildew_p3 rice:passageText """In the earlier stage of the disease, new leaves of infected plants produce chlorotic whitish spots or patches on the leaf blades, sometimes reducing them in size. At times, fluffy white to grey fungal growth may be seen on the under surface of the leaf blades. Infected young shoots and seeds may also show white coating of fungal growth."""@en .
rice:IRRI_RD2018_downy-mildew_p4 rice:passageText """In severe cases, the leaves may be distorted or twisted. The symptoms are more pronounced at flowering time. The panicles appear irregular, partially emerged from the leaf sheath freely and become contorted and, sometimes, spiral (DM Figure 1). The leaf sheath may also be twisted. The emerged panicle is reduced in size with part of its floral parts modified into twisted tubular structures and usually remains green longer than normal."""@en .
rice:IRRI_RD2018_grain-rot_p1 rice:passageText """As early as 1955, bacterial GnR was observed in Kyushu, Japan (Goto and Ohata 1956). Subsequently, it has been reported in Taiwan (Chien et al 1963), Korea in 1995 (Cha 1993, Song and Hyung Moo 1999), and mainland China in 2007 (Luo et al 2007). In the southern United States, bacterial panicle blight similar to grain rot had been reported in the early 1990s (Nandakumar et al 2009). Initially, the symptoms were thought to be caused by an abiotic stress related to high temperature, water, or toxic chemicals. The malady was later confirmed to be of bacterial origin caused by B. glumae and B. gladioli and identical to grain rot reported in Japan (Shahjahan et al 2000). It is then possible that other disease problems exist that may be attributed to nonbiological factors. In the United States, the disease is called bacterial panicle blight but in other countries the disease is known as grain rot."""@en .
rice:IRRI_RD2018_grain-rot_p2 rice:passageText """In Japan, GnR was initially regarded as a minor problem. However, since the 1970s, the incidence and severity continued to intensify in southern and central Japan (Uematsu et al 1976a,b), which coincided with the time when the conventional field nursery had been replaced by the nursery box for raising seedlings. In mid-1970s, the first incidence of B. glumae infecting seedlings in a nursery box was observed, showing that the bacterial pathogen attacks rice both at the seedling and panicle development stages (Uematsu et al 1976a,b). The importance of B. glumae as a rice bacterial pathogen has since been recognized. B. glumae has since been manifested as grain rot of mature plants at the heading stage in the field and as seedling rot in nursery boxes. However, seedling rot seldom occurs in field nursery. While examining the bacterial species associated with grain or sheath rot in a large number of seed samples from 22 different countries, Zeigler and Alvarez (1990) noted that Pseudomonas glumae (syn. B. glumae) among the four frequently encountered bacterial seed pathogens with a worldwide distribution."""@en .
rice:IRRI_RD2018_grain-rot_p3 rice:passageText """The disease manifests itself into distinct types of symptoms on rice plants (Sayler et al 2006; Uematsu et al 1976a,b). At the seedling stage in a nursery box, it is seedling rot while on grains in fields, it is grain rot. On leaf blades, especially flag leaves, the disease appears as small tan lesions measuring 1 to 5 mm with brown margins. On the leaf sheath, the lesion may be several centimeters long with a tan center and reddish brown margins on individual flag leaf sheaths of infected tillers. Infection occurs on spikelets causing kernel abortion before the kernels are completely filled. Infected florets may show dark gray or brown bases, but the rachis of the panicles remains green. GnR is usually realized when the infected seeds are sown for germination in nursery boxes. Panicles of infected tillers remain erect instead of bending due to loss of grain weight."""@en .
rice:IRRI_RD2018_grain-rot_p4 rice:passageText """The infection of seedlings results in seedling rot, likely derived from seedborne inoculum. Seed infestation or infection increases during presowing and water soaking or when seedlots carry the bacterial inoculum. Preemergence “damping off” causes GnR where the seeds fail to germinate and also forms a source of inoculum for other germinated seeds of disease foci in nursery boxes. The symptoms on seedlings caused by inoculum from rotting seeds usually develop into brown lesions on the coleoptile (Uematsu et al 1976a,b), which can cause seedling mortality. Lesions on infected and developed seedlings show brown, water-soaked soft rot of the leaf sheath accompanied by wilting or soft rot of the leaves (GnR Figure 1)."""@en .
rice:IRRI_RD2018_grain-rot_p5 rice:passageText """On grain rot, the panicles are shrunken and pale green, becoming dirty yellow to brown and dry. The symptoms may appear on the glumes and kernels. However, in Japan, panicle rachises and branches do not show symptoms. The hulls of infected grains on young panicles rapidly fade in color from the base, turning greenish white at first and then pale pink to yellowish brown. They soon wither and dry up. The infected grains are either sterile or carry brown rice with dark-brown discoloration at the base. A brown margin between the infected and healthy parts of the grain is a diagnostic feature of the disease (GnR Figure 2). The bacterium may cause mild rot of the flag leaf sheath of the leaf sheath collar."""@en .
rice:IRRI_RD2018_leaf-scald_p1 rice:passageText """The disease has three different names: leaf tip drying in China by Wei (1934), who was the first to describe the disease; leaf tip blight by Inoue and Takeda (1957), who suspected the causal organism to be the same agent of brown leaf blight; and LSc, given by Hashioka and Ikegami (1955)."""@en .
rice:IRRI_RD2018_leaf-scald_p2 rice:passageText """LSc is common on rice plants in Latin America (De Gutierrez 1960), and Asia. It has also been reported in the USA (Rush 1973) and West Africa (Lamey and Williams 1972). The disease had caused considerable losses in Latin America and was “sometimes as bad as rice blast” in West Africa (Lamey and Williams 1972)."""@en .
rice:IRRI_RD2018_leaf-scald_p3 rice:passageText """In tropical Asia, LSc sometimes occurs on rice leaves concomitantly with bacterial blight caused by Xanthomonas oryzae pv. oryzae. This appears to indicate that infection may be predisposed by wounds or the fungal pathogen invades the host tissue via injury or necrotic tissues. Thus, leaf clipping appears to be an effective method of producing the disease (Ou et al 1978)."""@en .
rice:IRRI_RD2018_leaf-scald_p4 rice:passageText """The lesions are characterized by zonation starting from the leaf tip or edges (Hashioka and Ikegami 1955), and usually occur on mature leaves. They are more or less oblong with light brown halos. Individual lesions are 1-5 cm long, 0.5-1 cm wide. The continuous enlargement and coalescing of lesions may result in blight on a large part of the leaf blade; lesions as long as 25 cm have been observed. The zonation fades as the lesions become old. Lesion without distinct zonation is called leaf tip blight in Japan, and leaf tip drying in China (Ou 1985) (LSc Figure 1)."""@en .
rice:IRRI_RD2018_leaf-scald_p5 rice:passageText """In Japan, brown leaf blight typically shows small, scattered, reddish-brown spots without necrotic tissues and with an indistinct margin. Leaf sheath browning is characterized by relatively large patches of brown lesions without necrosis and indistinct margins. Both types of symptoms are only occasionally observed in the tropics. The symptoms on the leaf sheath are easily confused with those of other diseases."""@en .
rice:IRRI_RD2018_leaf-scald_p6 rice:passageText """In Korea, besides the typical leaf symptoms, small, reddish-brown spots on the leaves (brown leaf blight) and long, elliptical or rectangular, purplish-brown or light grey were also described (Kwon et al 1973). Lesions on the neck of the panicles, similar to those on the leaf sheath, have also been observed (Kwon et al 1973). Von Thϋmen (cf. Wei 1934) indicated that the grains were infected. De Gutierrez (1960) reported a head blight causing considerable sterility, flower deformation and discoloration of glumes in Costa Rica. She described the fungus as causing decay of coleoptiles with red brown infection and root rot."""@en .
